WEBVTT

00:00:00.196 --> 00:00:05.158
Photograph a doorway from twice as far and
every structure is half as wide in pixels.

00:00:05.158 --> 00:00:09.271
A detector with one fixed scale sees
different things in each photo.

00:00:09.271 --> 00:00:14.283
So look at every scale, and let the image
say which scale belongs to each structure.

00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:17.696
That stack of ever more blurred copies is
the scale space.

00:00:17.696 --> 00:00:20.758
And blurs compose, because variances add:

00:00:20.758 --> 00:00:24.646
blur by three and then by four, and you've
blurred by five.

00:00:24.628 --> 00:00:28.499
To find a blob's size, measure the
Laplacian at every scale,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.241
multiplied by sigma squared so that scales
compare fairly.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.986
For a blob of width three, the response
rises to one half at sigma three,

00:00:36.986 --> 00:00:38.416
then falls away again.

00:00:38.416 --> 00:00:41.316
The peak is the blob's characteristic
scale.

00:00:41.316 --> 00:00:45.103
A keypoint is a point that wins in
position and in scale.

00:00:45.062 --> 00:00:48.562
Now enlarge the image by two, so the blob
has width six.

00:00:48.562 --> 00:00:49.900
Where does the peak move?

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.412
Pause and predict.

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:54.664
The peak moves to six.

00:00:54.664 --> 00:00:58.676
Detection follows the scale of the image,
which is the whole point.

00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:01.831
Second derivatives at every scale are
expensive,

00:01:01.832 --> 00:01:04.790
so SIFT subtracts neighbouring blurs
instead.

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:08.039
This difference of Gaussians is already
scale-normalised:

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:11.464
at this blob it gives minus zero point
zero eight four,

00:01:11.465 --> 00:01:15.077
against a prediction of minus zero point
zero eight three.

00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:17.420
A keypoint has a position and a scale.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.570
To compare it across images, it needs a
description.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.745
SIFT builds one from gradients.

00:01:22.745 --> 00:01:25.295
Take one cell with these four gradients.

00:01:25.295 --> 00:01:28.607
Each votes for its direction, weighted by
its length.

00:01:28.607 --> 00:01:32.695
Four by four cells of eight bins give one
hundred and twenty-eight numbers.

00:01:32.695 --> 00:01:36.382
Divide by the length, and doubling the
contrast changes nothing.

00:01:36.297 --> 00:01:40.384
Every descriptor has a nearest neighbour,
even one with no true partner.

00:01:40.384 --> 00:01:41.534
So test it.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:45.159
Lowe's ratio test compares the best
distance with the runner-up.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:48.559
Point two against point five is a ratio of
point four:

00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:49.484
accept.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:52.647
Point three against point three five is
point eight six:

00:01:52.647 --> 00:01:55.580
reject, because two candidates look
equally good,

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:57.735
like repeated windows on a building.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:01.372
The mutual test adds that the best match
must choose you back.
